{
  "title": "History of Prospect Park, Brooklyn, New York City",
  "overview": "Prospect Park is a 526-acre (213 ha) public park in Brooklyn, New York City. Designed by Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux, it opened in 1867 and is considered one of their masterpieces, alongside Central Park. The park was built on land that includes the site of the Battle of Long Island (1776) and was intended to provide Brooklyn with a large, pastoral public park.",
  "timeline": [
    {
      "period": "Pre-colonial and colonial",
      "details": "The area was originally inhabited by Lenape Native Americans. Dutch and English colonists later settled the region, and the land that became Prospect Park was used for farms, estates, and small settlements. Mount Prospect, the park's highest point, offered a commanding view of the surrounding area."
    },
    {
      "period": "Revolutionary War",
      "details": "On August 27, 1776, the Battle of Long Island was fought partly in what is now Prospect Park. Battle Pass, a strategic gap in the hills, was a key site. The Maryland Monument in the park commemorates the Maryland troops who fought there."
    },
    {
      "period": "Creation of the park (1859-1867)",
      "details": "In 1859, the New York State Legislature authorized the creation of Prospect Park for the City of Brooklyn. Land was acquired in 1860. In 1865, Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux won a design competition for the park. Construction began in 1866, and the park opened to the public in 1867, though work continued for several years."
    },
    {
      "period": "Completion and early years (1867-1898)",
      "details": "Olmsted and Vaux's design featured a 90-acre Long Meadow, a 60-acre lake, and a wooded Ravine meant to evoke the Adirondacks. The park was largely completed by 1873. It became a popular destination for Brooklyn residents. In 1898, Brooklyn consolidated with New York City, and Prospect Park became part of the New York City park system."
    },
    {
      "period": "20th century",
      "details": "In the 1930s, Parks Commissioner Robert Moses added and renovated facilities, including playgrounds and the Prospect Park Zoo. The Wollman Rink opened in 1961. After World War II, the park faced neglect and deterioration, especially during the 1970s fiscal crisis. In 1975, Prospect Park was designated a National Historic Landmark and a New York City Scenic Landmark."
    },
    {
      "period": "Restoration and modern era (1980-present)",
      "details": "In 1980, the Prospect Park Alliance was founded to restore and manage the park in partnership with NYC Parks. The Alliance signed a management contract in 1987. Major restorations followed, including the Long Meadow, the Ravine, the Lake, and the Lakeside Center. In 2021, a statue of Shirley Chisholm was unveiled in the park. Today, Prospect Park remains a vital green space for Brooklyn and New York City."
    }
  ],
  "designers": [
    "Frederick Law Olmsted",
    "Calvert Vaux"
  ],
  "size": "526 acres (213 hectares)",
  "location": "Brooklyn, New York City, New York, USA",
  "key_features": [
    "Long Meadow",
    "The Ravine",
    "Prospect Park Lake",
    "Boathouse",
    "Litchfield Villa",
    "Lefferts Historic House",
    "Prospect Park Zoo",
    "Wollman Rink",
    "Brooklyn Botanic Garden (adjacent)"
  ],
  "designations": [
    "National Historic Landmark (1975)",
    "New York City Scenic Landmark (1975)",
    "National Register of Historic Places (1975)"
  ],
  "legacy": "Prospect Park is widely regarded as one of the finest examples of 19th-century American landscape architecture. It influenced the design of many later parks and remains a model of public park management through the public-private partnership of the Prospect Park Alliance and NYC Parks."
}
```

